<%# locals: () %>
<%# The status row of the filter (Handout §3.3): the three billing states as
    CHECKBOXES with their hours, then "nur meine Zeiten".

    ⚠️ Checkboxes, not pills, since the layout of 02.09.2026. The three states
    are a SET the reader adds to and takes from; as filled pills they looked
    like the four choice surfaces above them, which ask a different question
    ("which one") than this row does ("which of these three").

    ⚠️ Still its own partial with an id, because it must be able to ARRIVE ON
    ITS OWN. The row sits outside time_sessions_list_frame (it is inside the
    filter panel, above the list), and the search targets that frame. Turbo
    swaps the frame and leaves everything around it alone -- so before this
    partial existed, typing a search that matched nothing left three figures
    standing beside a list saying "0 Zeilen".
    Worse, `state` below is decided once per render: a box that was empty on
    page load stayed a dead <span>, so a search whose only hits were invoiced
    could not be reached at all -- the one case the "an active box stays
    clickable" rule was written for.
    _index_table sends this partial along with every frame answer. See the
    turbo_stream element at its foot.

    ⚠️ NO locals: every one of TimeSessionsHelper::FRAME_DEPENDENT_REGIONS reads
    its own ivars, which is what lets that list stay data instead of four
    hand-written turbo_stream.replace blocks with four different signatures.

    query_toggle semantics stay: the href is the no-JS fallback, `owns` is the
    JS path (lib/query_toggle.js). %>
<%# ⚠️ Nothing to say without counts, and one path renders this table without
    them: create.turbo_stream, whose list is unfiltered. A region of
    FRAME_DEPENDENT_REGIONS decides for ITSELF whether it has an answer -- the
    list stays data, and the condition sits where the knowledge is. %>
<% return if @status_counts.blank? %>
<% status_counts = @status_counts %>
<% effective_status_filters = @effective_status_filters || [] %>
<div id="time_status_pills" class="flex items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 flex-wrap">
  <% TimeSession::BILLING_STATUS_FILTERS.each do |value| %>
    <% label_text = time_billing_state_label(value) %>
    <% active = effective_status_filters.include?(value) %>
    <% bucket = status_counts.fetch(value) %>
    <%# Emptiness is decided on the BOOKING count, not on the minutes. The two
        cannot disagree today (TimeSession validates duration > 0), but the
        question "is there anything here" is about bookings, and the day a
        zero-minute booking becomes possible, greying its box would hide the one
        row that could explain the zero. %>
    <% duration = TimeSession.format_duration(bucket[:minutes]) %>
    <%# An empty state is not worth a click, so its box stops being one. But an
        ACTIVE box always stays clickable even at zero: it is the way back out of
        the filter that emptied the list, and a page that shows nothing and
        offers no way to undo it is a trap. %>
    <% state = if active
                 :on
               elsif bucket[:sessions].positive?
                 :off
               else
                 :empty
               end %>
    <% unit = t('views.organisations.time_sessions.index_table.sum_unit') %>

    <% body = capture do %>
      <%= time_status_box(state) %>
      <span class="<%= time_status_word_classes(state) %>"><%= label_text %></span>
      <span class="<%= time_status_figure_classes(state) %>"><%= duration %></span>
    <% end %>

    <% if state == :empty %>
      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2" data-status-box="<%= value %>" data-status-state="empty">
        <%= body %>
        <%# sr-only rather than aria-label + title: aria-label on a <span> with no
            role is invalid per ARIA 1.2 and may be dropped, and a title is only
            reachable with a mouse. This says the unit AND the reason, to everyone. %>
        <span class="sr-only"><%= "#{duration} #{unit}. #{t('views.organisations.time_sessions.index.filter_status_none_here')}" %></span>
      </span>
    <% else %>
      <%= link_to time_list_filter_url('status_filters' => time_status_filter_toggle(value, effective_status_filters)),
                  data: time_filter_owns(time_status_filter_owns_pairs(value, effective_status_filters))
                          .merge(status_box: value, status_state: state),
                  aria: { current: (active ? 'true' : nil) },
                  class: TimeSessionsHelper::STATUS_ROW_LINK do %>
        <%= body %>
        <span class="sr-only"><%= "#{duration} #{unit}" %></span>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# "Only my sessions" -- the same checkbox language, because it is the same
      kind of question. It carries no figure: it is not one of the three buckets
      the hours are split into.
      Shown only where it can change something: for someone who may see only
      their own hours it would filter the list down to itself. %>
  <% if shows_foreign_time? %>
    <%# Compared in the one spelling (ListFilters.normalize_id): the list narrows
        to a capitalised own id as well, so the switch has to light for it. %>
    <% mine_active = TimeSessions::ListFilters.normalize_id(params[:creator_id]) == current_user.id %>
    <%= link_to time_list_filter_url('creator_id' => mine_active ? nil : current_user.id),
                data: time_filter_owns([['creator_id', mine_active ? '' : current_user.id]])
                        .merge(status_box: 'creator_mine'),
                aria: { current: (mine_active ? 'true' : nil) },
                class: TimeSessionsHelper::STATUS_ROW_LINK do %>
      <%= time_status_box(mine_active ? :on : :off) %>
      <span class="<%= time_status_word_classes(mine_active ? :on : :off) %>">
        <%# No :default. A fallback here is a second place the word can live, and
            this row has exactly one owner for every word in it. %>
        <%= t('views.organisations.time_sessions.index.filter_only_mine') %>
      </span>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>
